[39;49m[K[2m• [22mComputer terminals began as electromechanical teleprinters that let operators send commands to large shared computers[39m[49m[0m
[39;49m[K  and receive printed output. By the 1960s and 1970s, video display terminals replaced paper output with screens, making[39m[49m[0m
[39;49m[K  interactive computing faster and more practical. Popular devices such as the DEC VT100 helped standardize terminal[39m[49m[0m
[39;49m[K  behavior, including control codes and cursor movement. As personal computers spread, terminal emulators recreated[39m[49m[0m
[39;49m[K  those hardware interfaces in software for connecting to remote systems and command-line environments. Today, terminals[39m[49m[0m
